WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.485 --> 00:00:02.539
[ice clicking]

00:00:02.539 --> 00:00:04.630
[quiet thudding]

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.970
Narrator: Look at these two paper bags.

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:09.870
They don't appear very different,

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:11.767
but they sound different.

00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:14.483
One of these is a real,
everyday paper bag,

00:00:14.483 --> 00:00:16.733
[bag rustling]

00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:19.738
and the other one is a silent prop.

00:00:19.738 --> 00:00:24.000
[bag quietly unfolding]

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.170
This fake paper bag was an innovation

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:28.760
thought up by prop maker Tim Schultz.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:30.940
Props is always about solving a problem.

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:32.210
Narrator: And one of those problems

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:35.050
is everyday objects that
don't look distracting,

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:36.995
but sound distracting.

00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:39.385
I often say that a sound guy can hear

00:00:39.385 --> 00:00:41.440
grass growing when you're on location.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.570
Narrator: Silencing
some props, like drums,

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:46.212
can be fairly simple,
like adding a drum mute.

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:47.095
[drum booms]

00:00:47.095 --> 00:00:47.928
[muted drum thuds]

00:00:47.928 --> 00:00:49.152
[drum booms]
[muted drum thuds]

00:00:49.152 --> 00:00:53.390
But others require getting
a bit more creative,

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:55.850
like with pool balls, which were too loud

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:58.040
for these two bar scenes in season one

00:00:58.040 --> 00:00:59.615
of "Friday Night Lights."

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:02.630
Man: Nice shot!

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:04.160
Narrator: Property master Scott Reeder

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:05.770
needed to find a ball that would create

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:07.970
a softer sound on impact.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:11.140
First, he experimented with
plastic balls from a ball pit.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.600
They were much quieter,

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.950
but he couldn't get paint
to stick to the surface.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.530
Then he remembered a trick he used

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:20.300
on 1991's "Necessary Roughness"

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.060
during a shot where a character is thrown

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:24.190
onto a pool table in a bar fight.

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:26.960
Reeder swapped real pool
balls with racquetballs,

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.140
so the actor would land
on something softer.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.420
We didn't really clue in at the time

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:34.360
that, oh, you know, these
would be good for sound.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.412
Narrator: Listen to how
real pool balls sound.

00:01:36.412 --> 00:01:37.920
[balls clacking]

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.160
It's too noisy.

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:40.985
Narrator: Compared to the racquetballs.

00:01:40.985 --> 00:01:42.718
[quiet thudding]

00:01:42.718 --> 00:01:43.730
Much better.

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:47.610
Narrator: To get it to
look like the real thing,

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.177
Reeder added a layer of primer

00:01:49.177 --> 00:01:50.443
to make sure the paint stuck,

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:51.926
followed with a glossy spray

00:01:51.926 --> 00:01:54.520
to give off the signature
shine of a real pool ball.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.610
Reeder: You gotta pick them up

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:57.750
to tell that they're not real.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.090
Narrator: And Reeder also had to know

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:00.757
when to swap the props in.

00:02:00.757 --> 00:02:02.930
Reeder: If actors are playing in a scene,

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:04.890
we'll typically do real

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:07.530
if the camera's going to
see them hit the ball.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.410
And then when the camera goes

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:11.400
to where it's just on their face,

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.360
we'll throw the fake ones on the table

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.467
so they can actually play pool.

00:02:15.467 --> 00:02:19.280
That way, the sound man
can catch the dialogue.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:20.960
Narrator: Prop masters don't always need

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:22.550
to find a replacement.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:23.975
Some silent props are made

00:02:23.975 --> 00:02:26.041
by just modifying the real thing.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:29.080
For example, in this scene in "Tammy,"

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.043
actress Melissa McCarthy needed to wear

00:02:31.043 --> 00:02:33.430
a paper bag on her head
to hide her identity

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:35.900
and another one on her hand to hide a gun.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:39.320
Schultz said it was important
for her to use the real thing,

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.899
but a real paper bag would
sound something like this.

00:02:41.899 --> 00:02:43.877
[paper rustling]
Tammy: You want some pies?

00:02:43.877 --> 00:02:44.737
[paper rustling]
Worker: I like pie.

00:02:44.737 --> 00:02:45.871
[paper rustling]
Tammy: Well, get a pie,

00:02:45.871 --> 00:02:47.600
[paper rustling]
you get a pie for --

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.330
Narrator: Quieting these real bags down

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:51.160
meant adding more weight to them.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.620
So Schultz sprayed the bags

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.640
with a mix of water and glycerin,

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.470
which is typically used
to add the appearance

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:57.810
of sweat or water droplets

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.592
on anything from human
skin to beer bottles.

00:03:00.592 --> 00:03:03.330
Schultz: Takes quite a bit of --

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:04.588
well, it takes time and finesse,

00:03:04.588 --> 00:03:06.330
'cause you don't want to overdo it.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.040
Narrator: He also taped the bags inside

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.720
to hold them together and
prevent them from crumbling.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.610
But this fix was only temporary,

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:14.660
since the bags would
eventually lose their color

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:16.180
and fall apart over time.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:17.760
And this wouldn't work for when actors

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:19.857
have to handle bags consistently.

00:03:19.857 --> 00:03:21.770
So for other projects, Schultz developed

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:23.690
a quiet, durable, alternative

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:26.220
using fibrous nonwoven fabric.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:29.450
This is actually the same
material as coffee filters.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:30.820
To make it look like the real thing,

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:33.150
Schultz wraps the fabric around a mold,

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:35.540
then he glues and folds the sides by hand.

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:36.935
Schultz: We're trying to copy the bottom

00:03:36.935 --> 00:03:41.920
of a standard bag that's
really made by a machine.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.180
It's important to get the
corners so that they're square.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.000
A lot of times we'll,
when we're using them,

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.440
we'll crinkle them and
make them look more used,

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:54.290
so they have a more realistic look.

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:56.530
Instead of cutting it smooth,

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:59.100
it's going to cut it with
a little jagged edge to it.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.140
Narrator: This fabric can be used

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:01.800
on other paper products as well,

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:03.870
like when this butcher
in "The Kominsky Method"

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:05.470
talks to Alan Arkin while grabbing

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:07.240
a handful of cold cuts.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.140
That butcher paper?

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:10.320
Fake.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:11.153
[paper rustling]

00:04:11.153 --> 00:04:12.928
It's these small handheld props

00:04:12.928 --> 00:04:14.950
that give prop masters the most trouble,

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:17.050
especially in scenes with dialogue.

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:18.810
For example, an actor with a drink

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:21.060
will likely hold it near their midsection,

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.030
where microphones are often attached.

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:24.526
It's especially important to make sure

00:04:24.526 --> 00:04:25.800
whatever is in that drink

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.270
doesn't produce any unwanted sounds.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:30.340
So if you're watching
a show like "Entourage"

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:32.400
with lots of scenes at parties and bars,

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.663
it's safe to assume that
these ice cubes are all fake.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:38.542
But not all fake ice is the same.

00:04:38.542 --> 00:04:40.260
Plastic cubes don't melt,

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.246
which is great for continuity,

00:04:42.246 --> 00:04:44.323
but they're still really loud.

00:04:44.323 --> 00:04:45.840
[cubes clicking]

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.620
So when sound is crucial,

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.840
Schultz often opts for
another type of fake cubes.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:53.199
These ones, made out of silicone.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.700
These rubber cubes can
either be molded in trays

00:04:58.700 --> 00:04:59.830
or ripped apart.

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:01.930
Since the final product floats in a glass,

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:03.502
they look more convincing.

00:05:03.502 --> 00:05:05.870
[ice softly clunking]

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:08.543
And, best of all, they're
completely silent.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.600
Schultz: Background actors
can move this stuff around

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.880
without having to worry about the sound.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:18.580
Narrator: So when this waitress walks by

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:20.200
with a trayful of cups with ice,

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:21.290
you can hear ...

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:22.180
Turtle: What time's dinner?

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:23.070
We're starving.

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:24.137
Narrator: And not ...

00:05:24.137 --> 00:05:25.188
[ice clinking]
Turtle: What time's dinner?

00:05:25.188 --> 00:05:26.220
[ice clinking]
We're starving.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:28.850
Narrator: While ice cubes only
pop up in certain moments,

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:31.529
other loud materials are everywhere.

00:05:31.529 --> 00:05:32.770
Like plastic.

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:35.410
Everything from sandwich
bags to potato-chip bags

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:36.950
make loud crackles.

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:39.430
So Schultz will create
fake ones out of vinyl.

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:40.879
So, this material,

00:05:40.879 --> 00:05:42.079
[dull crackling]

00:05:42.079 --> 00:05:43.571
it's that dull sound.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:47.210
Narrator: And it comes in handy

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:49.150
for other places you might not expect,

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:51.660
like flower bouquets that
are wrapped in cellophane.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:53.520
Take this moment from
"The Kominsky Method"

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:54.930
where Michael Douglas' character

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:56.910
brings flowers to an ailing friend.

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:59.440
If Schultz had used real
cellophane for the bouquet,

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:01.166
all the sound team would hear is ...

00:06:01.166 --> 00:06:01.999
[plastic crackling]
What kind of flowers

00:06:01.999 --> 00:06:02.914
[plastic crackling]
are those?

00:06:02.914 --> 00:06:04.400
[plastic crackling]
I have no idea.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:06.400
Narrator: So, he used a
transparent vinyl material

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.600
to replicate the look
without all the noise.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.250
What kind of flowers are those?

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:13.020
I have no idea.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:15.660
Uh-huh. So your daughter bought them?

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:16.560
Yeah.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:19.160
Narrator: But no solution
is one-size-fits-all.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:21.310
[tissue rustling]
Noisy tissue,

00:06:22.376 --> 00:06:23.784
but a quiet bag.

00:06:23.784 --> 00:06:25.970
[tissue rustling]

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:27.930
Still doesn't solve the problem.

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:29.750
Narrator: Some objects
need multiple layers

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:32.500
of silent material to
effectively kill any noise,

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.533
like with these gift bags.

00:06:34.533 --> 00:06:37.105
So, quiet tissue.

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:38.893
Quiet bag.

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:42.740
Done.

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:44.208
Narrator: So, next time you see a scene

00:06:44.208 --> 00:06:45.800
set in a grocery store, a bar,

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:47.700
or even just around the office,

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:49.220
look twice at the everyday objects

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:51.635
you can see, but can't hear.

00:06:59.757 --> 00:07:02.124
We can all thank the prop makers for that.

